    --[[
        What a resident is holding, keyed by the prop their definition
        names.

        This was two inline branches and an `else`, which is a fine shape
        for three people and stops being one at eleven: the `else` was the
        seedkeeper's basket, so every prop nobody had written a branch for
        would have come out as her green box. A smith carrying a basket
        says the wrong thing about the smith, and says it silently.

        Each prop is an assembly rather than one block, because one block
        is what a hammer, a ladle and a pair of shears all look like --
        the visual standard's rule is that recolouring a cube is not
        construction, and a prop held in the hand is the closest piece of
        art in the game to a player's eye when they walk up to talk to
        somebody. A hammer is a haft with a head on it; a ladle is a shaft
        with a bowl; shears are two blades on a pivot. The three that were
        already here are built the same way now, basket included, because
        the standard does not have a grandfather clause.
    ]]
    -- A Roblox cylinder lies on its side by default -- its flat faces are
    -- the two X faces -- so anything meant to stand up as a vessel has to
    -- be tipped a quarter turn first, and its size reads (height,
    -- diameter, diameter) afterwards.
    local UPRIGHT_CYLINDER = CFrame.Angles(0, 0, math.rad(90))
    --[[
        The one prop tall enough to reach past the elbow leans forward off
        the hand. Hung upright on a real R15 rig the shaft ran straight
        through the upper arm from the elbow up, because the grip sits
        only 0.3 in front of the forearm and the arm is 0.93 deep. A
        twenty-six degree lean carries the whole shaft clear of the front
        face of the arm, and a staff leaning off a hand is what a carried
        staff looks like anyway.
    ]]
    local STAFF_LEAN = CFrame.Angles(math.rad(-26), 0, 0)

    local RESIDENT_PROPS: { [string]: { ResidentPropPiece } } = {
        --[[
            Not a bell -- a bell's tongue, carried out of a valley that
            stopped answering, because the bell itself weighs half a ton
            and the part that made the sound does not.

            Odell already carries a bell, so this one has to read as
            something else at a glance: it hangs from a staple instead of
            standing on a crown, and the strike ball is worn back to
            bronze where the iron everywhere else is not.

            The first version photographed as a bolt. What fixed it was
            proportion rather than parts: the ball is now nearly four
            times the shaft it hangs on, the staple is a loop with real
            daylight inside it, and the flight runs a long way past the
            ball. Nothing else in the game has a heavy sphere with a thin
            tail hanging below it, and that is what has to be true of a
            silhouette before the nameplate is any use.
        ]]
        bell_clapper = {
            {
                color = Color3.fromRGB(78, 82, 86),
