        --[[
            A three-fluke grapple on the end of its line. Each fluke is an
            arm leaning out and a barb turning back up, and the three are
            the same pair spun a third of a turn apart -- which is why
            each offset opens with a yaw and then builds the fluke in the
            frame that yaw left behind.

            The splay is the whole silhouette. A grapple with its arms
            tucked in is an anchor, and an anchor is not what she does --
            but the first pass splayed thin dark arms straight out and
            down and photographed as a microphone stand. What a grapnel
            actually does is turn: the arm goes out, and then the barb
            comes back up, and the claw is that corner. The arms are
            heavier for it and the barbs are a brighter steel, so the
            turn survives being small.
        ]]
