        --[[
            A framing square: two flat steel arms meeting at a corner, one
            long and one short, with a wooden fence pinned along the short
            one.

            The first pass was a try-square -- a thick stock with a blade
            out of its face -- and it photographed as a ruler with a
            handle, because the stock read as the end of the long arm
            rather than as a second arm. What makes a square is that both
            arms are the same object turned ninety degrees, so they are
            the same steel and the same thickness now and the corner is
            where they meet rather than where one stops.

            The graduations are four and small. Nine of them striped the
            blade like a ladder and swallowed the shape. The red chalk
            mark is a long way past the last of them, which is where he
            actually cuts: he cut beams for a city that burned anyway, and
            he cuts them longer now.
        ]]
