            never his. The roster gives him `requiresEnding`, so he is
            standing here for `ending_ended` and `ending_shared` and is
            not for `ending_kept` -- because if the light was kept then
            somebody is still down there tending it, and it is him. That
            is one field rather than three copies of a town.

            Day faces him up the road at the First Lantern from his own
            door, which is the whole shape of the thing: he spent years
            underneath a light nobody could see, and the one he can see
            now is the town's rather than his. Dusk puts him at its foot
            while it is lit, close enough to touch and not touching it.

            Night is the part that is easy to get wrong. The obvious place
            for the keeper who kept the watch alone is the end of the
            road, on his own, and it is exactly wrong -- the thing that is
            different now is that there are twenty-six other people out
            here. He stands in the line, in the middle of it, at a post
            somebody would have to count to find.
